WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.532
Row grain is one of the most important design habits in this course.

00:00:05.532 --> 00:00:09.382
Grain answers the question: what does one row represent?

00:00:09.382 --> 00:00:13.007
For a patient table, one row may represent one patient.

00:00:13.007 --> 00:00:17.367
For appointment, one row may represent one scheduled encounter.

00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:21.077
For service, one row may represent one reusable service definition.

00:00:21.077 --> 00:00:26.887
For appointment service, one row may represent one service used during one appointment.

00:00:26.887 --> 00:00:32.917
Problems begin when one row tries to represent several different subjects at the same time.

00:00:32.917 --> 00:00:39.922
Before choosing columns for any table, finish the sentence: one row equals one what?

00:00:39.922 --> 00:00:45.612
If that sentence is hard to complete, the table design may be mixing several concepts and should be

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:48.797
reconsidered before implementation.

00:00:48.797 --> 00:00:54.792
Before drawing tables, write one sentence that defines what one row represents.

00:00:54.792 --> 00:00:58.352
That grain statement prevents many later problems.

00:00:58.352 --> 00:01:04.872
If one row represents a patient, repeated encounters do not belong as extra columns in the same row;

00:01:04.872 --> 00:01:08.000
they are a different entity with a one-to-many relationship.
